#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063914 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9722c125-2d26-5aed-ab60-2e2358a17fdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Discrete"}] :question/id #uuid "65a0be54-6801-5337-b68f-01c7049365cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-10088" :question/problem "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nLet $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{185}$ be a random sample of size $n = 185$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation in terms of $\\Phi(z)$ for $P(\\mid \\overline{X} \\mid \\gt 0.1)$.\n\n*Note: $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n$n = 185$,\n- the population mean is\n$μ|= E[X]; = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}f(x_{i}); =-3 \\cdot 0.4 + (-1) \\cdot 0.1 + 1 \\cdot 0.1 + 3 \\cdot 0.4; =-1.2 - 0.1 + 0.1 + 1.2; = 0$,\n- the population variance is\n$σ^{2}|= Var[X]; = E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2}; = ((-3)^{2} \\cdot 0.4 + (-1)^{2} \\cdot 0.1 + 1^{2} \\cdot 0.1 + 3^{2} \\cdot 0.4) - 0^{2}; = (3.6 + 0.1 + 0.1 + 3.6) - 0; = 7.4$.\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\nX^(―) \\mid ∼N(μ, \\frac{σ^{2}}{n}); ∼N(0, \\frac{7.4}{185}); ∼N(0, 0.04)\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\mid \\overline{X} \\mid \\gt 0.1)$. So, we convert $\\overline{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\mid X^(―) \\mid > 0.1) &= P(X^(―) < - 0.1) + P(X^(―) > 0.1) \\\\\n&\\approx P(Z < \\frac{-0.1 - 0}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) + P(Z > \\frac{0.1 - 0}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) \\\\\n&= P(Z < - 0.5) + P(Z > 0.5) \\\\\n&= P(Z < - 0.5) + 1 - P(Z < 0.5) \\\\\n&= 1 + Φ(-0.5) - Φ(0.5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\qquad \\Phi(-0.5) = 1-\\Phi(0.5)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\mid X^(―) \\mid > 0.1) &= 1 + Φ(-0.5) - Φ(0.5) \\\\\n&= 1 + (1 - Φ(0.5)) - Φ(0.5) \\\\\n&= 2 - 2Φ(0.5) \\\\\n&= 2(1 - Φ(0.5))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063915 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327e406f-f432-5db4-a79f-80195c02cc96" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Continuous"}] :question/id #uuid "a31b1e5c-c4e3-57df-9e05-5f2fdc6b3397" :question/math-academy-id "e-10090" :question/problem "Let $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{100}$ be a random sample of size $n=100$ from a population with probability density function\n${\\frac{2}{9}x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nGiven that the population variance $\\sigma^2 = 0.5$, calculate $P(\\overline{X}>1.9)$.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, -1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0655 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0643 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0630 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0793 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0778 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0764 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0951 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0934 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0918 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n$n|= 100$,\n- the population mean is\n$μ|= E[X]; = \\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}xf(x)dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}x \\cdot \\frac{2}{9}xdx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\frac{2}{9}x^{2}dx; = [\\frac{2x^{3}}{27}]_{0}^{3}; = 2$,\n- the population variance is $\\sigma^2 = 0.5$.\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\nX^(―) \\mid ∼N(μ, \\frac{σ^{2}}{n}); ∼N(2, \\frac{0.5}{100}); ∼N(2, 0.005)\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overline{X}>1.9)$. So, we convert $\\overline{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X^(―) > 1.9) &= P(Z > \\frac{1.9 - 2}{\\sqrt{0.005}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(Z > - 1.41)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z > - 1.41) &= 1 - P(Z \\le - 1.41) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(-1.41)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-1.41) = 0.0793\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X^(―) > 1.9) &= 1 - Φ(-1.41) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.0793 \\\\\n&= 0.9207\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064140 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b835315-d2fd-5823-9fb6-0f5535d3d549" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the Central Limit Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "238819c6-7c7b-5b4b-ab82-ba43e2ac1c97" :question/math-academy-id "e-9197" :question/problem "Let $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{120}$ be a random sample of size $n = 120$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu = 100$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma = 60$. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $102$ and $105$.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$$\\, 0.07 \\,$$\\, 0.08 \\,$$\\, 0.09 \\,$$\\, 0.2 \\,$ $\\, 0.6064 \\,$ $\\, 0.6103 \\,$ $\\, 0.6141 \\,$ $\\, 0.3 \\,$ $\\, 0.6443 \\,$ $\\, 0.6480 \\,$ $\\, 0.6517 \\,$ $\\, 0.4 \\,$ $\\, 0.6808 \\,$ $\\, 0.6844 \\,$ $\\, 0.6879 \\,$ |  | $z$$\\, 0.00 \\,$$\\, 0.01 \\,$$\\, 0.02 \\,$$\\, 0.7 \\,$ $\\, 0.7580 \\,$ $\\, 0.7611 \\,$ $\\, 0.7642 \\,$ $\\, 0.8 \\,$ $\\, 0.7881 \\,$ $\\, 0.7910 \\,$ $\\, 0.7939 \\,$ $\\, 0.9 \\,$ $\\, 0.8159 \\,$ $\\, 0.8186 \\,$ $\\, 0.8212 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn = 120, \\qquad \\mu = 100, \\qquad \\sigma = 60\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\nX^(―) \\mid ∼N(μ, \\frac{σ^{2}}{n}); ∼N(100, \\frac{60^{2}}{120}); ∼N(100, 30)\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(102 \\lt \\overline{X} \\lt 105)$. So, we convert $\\overline{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(102 < X^(―) < 105) &= P(\\frac{102 - 100}{\\sqrt{30}} < Z < \\frac{105 - 100}{\\sqrt{30}}) \\\\\n&= P(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} < Z < \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(0.37 < Z < 0.91) \\\\\n&= P(Z < 0.91) - P(Z < 0.37) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.91) - Φ(0.37)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.37) = 0.6443,; Φ(0.91) = 0.8186\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(102 < X^(―) < 105) &= Φ(0.91) - Φ(0.37) \\\\\n&= 0.8186 - 0.6443 \\\\\n&= 0.1743\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{